# Code owners for local-operator-ui.
#
# THERE ARE NO PATTERNS IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. No line here starts
# with a path or `*`; a matching pattern is the only thing that makes GitHub
# create a review request, so nothing is requested automatically when a PR opens.
#
# 2026-09-16 -- AUTO-REQUESTS REMOVED (operator decision). The `*` line that
# used to sit at the bottom of this file (`@damianvtran @bbqben`) existed only to
# route: GitHub requests every handle on a pattern matching a pull request's
# changed files, automatically, at the moment the PR opens -- so `bbqben` was
# notified on every change in this repository, including the ones still churning
# through review rounds and QA. There is no GitHub setting that keeps the routing
# list and drops the notification; matching a pattern IS the request. The pattern
# is gone, and this path is kept, pattern-free, as the record of the change.
#
# 2026-09-22 -- THE ENFORCEMENT HALF WAS WRITTEN, AND IT DID NOT PUT A PATTERN
# BACK. A ruleset named `Main Protection` is configured for the default branch,
# mirroring the backend repository's (`local-operator`, ruleset 3622629): a
# `non_fast_forward` rule, plus a `pull_request` rule with
# `required_approving_review_count: 1`, `require_code_owner_review: false`,
# `required_review_thread_resolution: false`, `dismiss_stale_reviews_on_push:
# false`, `require_last_push_approval: false`,
# `require_extra_approval_for_unattributed_changes: true` (GitHub's *Require an
# additional approval for unattributed Copilot pull requests*: it is scoped to a
# pull request COPILOT opened under its own app identity rather than on behalf of
# a person, not to an ordinary PR), and
# `allowed_merge_methods: [merge, squash, rebase]`; plus one bypass actor,
# `RepositoryRole` id 5 with `bypass_mode: always`. On a repository owned by a
# personal account that role belongs to the OWNER ALONE: such a repository has
# exactly two permission levels, the owner and collaborators, and GitHub refuses
# `admin` and `maintain` on one (`422 Cannot assign <user> permission of admin`),
# so the bypass is the owner's and no collaborator's (GitHub, *Permission levels
# for a personal account repository*). The same page names the escape hatch if
# those roles are ever wanted: transferring the repository to an organization is
# what creates them, and what would let a team-scoped bypass actor exist. The
# ruleset IS attached: created 2026-09-22 as id `23841604`, and
# `gh api .../rules/branches/main` (the second command below) returns it today.
# Diff these sentences against the repository rather than trusting them:
#
#   gh api repos/damianvtran/local-operator-ui/rulesets
#   gh api repos/damianvtran/local-operator-ui/rules/branches/main
#
# `require_code_owner_review: false` is what lets this file stay pattern-free.
# GitHub scopes that rule to content that HAS a code owner -- "any pull request
# that modifies content with a code owner must be approved by that code owner
# before the pull request can be merged into the protected branch" -- so with no
# paths declared it would be VACUOUS rather than a hazard: nothing here has an
# owner, so the rule would never demand an approval from anyone and no merge
# would be forced through the bypass. Turning it ON would therefore buy exactly no
# control, which is why the setting is `false`; the review *count* is what carries
# the protection. The count is the tier-2 rule in
# AGENTS.md, "Who may merge: two tiers": an outside contributor's PR cannot reach
# `main` without an approving review from a collaborator other than its author.
# Before 2026-09-22 `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/rules/branches/main` answered
# `[]` here -- no ruleset was attached -- so nothing mechanical stood behind those
# rules; with the ruleset attached the approval count and the no-force-push rule
# do, and the agent-review discipline is unchanged either way.
#
# The owner set -- whose judgement stands in for the project's, and who may be
# asked for a human review when one is genuinely wanted -- is `damianvtran`, who
# owns this repository and is its only administrator; `bbqben`, who holds write
# access today; and `olafagbemi`, `sherman-tsui` and `SanaKetabchi`, who hold it
# by INVITATION sent 2026-09-22 and hold nothing until they accept it. Members of
# the set are not administrators and cannot be: a repository owned by a personal
# account has exactly two permission levels, the owner and collaborators (see the
# section above). Write access is precisely what makes the
# set effective -- an approving review from any write-access collaborator other
# than the author satisfies the ruleset's review count, and "act as a code owner"
# is a collaborator capability on such a repository. `jcobhams` holds write access
# too but is deliberately NOT in that set: ownership here means "can approve on
# behalf of the project", which is narrower than commit access. Verify a handle
# before relying on it with
# `gh api repos/damianvtran/local-operator-ui/collaborators` -- `bbqben` and
# `jcobhams` hold write while `olafagbemi`, `sherman-tsui` and `SanaKetabchi` hold
# it by invitation sent 2026-09-22 and take it on acceptance, which is why the
# endpoint is read rather than this sentence -- since a handle without write
# access is silently ignored by GitHub. The review count is satisfied by an
# approving review from *any* collaborator other than the author, not only from
# the set above, because no code owners are declared.
#
# The self-approval limitation is the thing to read before adding a pattern
# back: GitHub refuses `422 Review Can not approve your own pull request`, and
# every agent here pushes as the owner's account, so an agent-authored PR the
# owner opened can never be clicked approved by the account that opened it. That
# is why the owner's bypass -- the ruleset's one bypass actor, which only the
# owner can hold -- is the *sanctioned* completion for the owner's own reviewed
# work here rather than a hole, and why it must be disclosed on the PR
# (AGENTS.md, "Who may merge: two tiers"; the backend's section of the same name,
# and its "How the forge records tier 1", are the long form).
#
# When a human review IS wanted it is a deliberate act rather than a side effect
# of opening a PR: add the reviewer by hand at the point the PR is ready to be
# read, `gh pr edit <n> --add-reviewer <handle>` (no leading `@` -- gh <= 2.100
# only strips it for `@me` and `@copilot`). Tagging a person in a *comment* is
# what says the PR is waiting on them, so neither is done casually.
#
# Validate any future edit with GitHub's own parser rather than by eye, and pin
# the ref or it silently validates the default branch instead of your branch:
# `gh api 'repos/damianvtran/local-operator-ui/codeowners/errors?ref=<branch>'`
# (an empty error list means the file parsed).
